{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in those many days.</b> That Moshe was fleeing from Pharaoh. <b>And the king of Egypt died, and the children of Israel groaned from the labor.</b> The death of the king grieved them even though he was wicked, out of their fear lest a worse wicked man arise after him. And it says <b>and they cried out from the oppression</b> that was done to them, in the manner of what is written (Job 35:9): ״מרוב עשוקים יזעיקו״. And it says <b>and their cry rose up to Elohim from the labor.</b> For even though the appointed end had already arrived, they were not worthy of redemption except that because they cried out abundantly to HaShem from the labor, He accepted their prayer. And He mentioned 'from the labor' twice: 'and the children of Israel groaned from the labor' and 'and their cry rose up to Elohim from the labor,' to teach you that a person's prayer is not whole like that prayer which he prays from within the trouble and distress, which is more acceptable and which is what rises before Him, blessed be He. And so you find with Yonah the prophet, peace be upon him, that he explained this very matter, saying (Jonah 2:8): ״בהתעטף עלי נפשי את ה׳ זכרתי ותבא אליך תפלתי אל היכל קדשך״ — the prophet assured that the prayer which comes from within suffering and the enveloping of the soul is what enters before Him into the sanctuary of His holiness, blessed be He." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that this section is an allusion to our coming redemption, which depends on repentance and prayer. For so in the redemption from Egypt they repented and prayed to HaShem, who answers in time of trouble, and their prayer was accepted and the redeemer came to them immediately. And therefore it is joined immediately with 'and Moshe,' to allude that the coming of the redeemer depends on repentance and prayer." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah</b> it is possible to interpret that the matter of prayer is alluded to here, and from within the distress and the magnitude of the bondage they directed their intention to the ten and to the head and to the end, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 58:9): ״אז תקרא וה׳ יענה״. And therefore all of them are alluded to here, as it says: 'and their cry rose up to Elohim' — 'and Elohim heard' — 'and Elohim remembered,' corresponding to fear (יראה), wisdom (חכמה), and understanding (בינה). 'Avraham, Yitzhak, and Yaakov' — greatness (גדולה), might (גבורה), and foundation (יסוד), and the two pillars are included within them. 'And Elohim saw' corresponds to beauty (תפארת), and 'Elohim knew' corresponds to kingdom (מלכות). Set your heart to the fact that He did not mention 'the children of Israel' but with 'and Elohim saw,' in order to allude to the beauty (תפארת) of Israel, for there the children of Israel cry out and there they pray in the request for their needs, and to Him we bless in the Kaddish: 'Amen, may His great name be blessed,' and we say 'for ever and ever and for all eternity' (לעלם לעלמי עלמיא) — 'let Him be blessed' (יתברך): He mentioned HaShem, worlds (עולמות), corresponding to the five sefirot from there and above, and one must join the word עלמיא to יתברך and not make any separation between them at all, just as one may not separate the Crown (כתר) from the Infinite (אין סוף). Hear now, and you shall know for yourself."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {2 "The phrase 'העשר ואל הראש ועד הסוף' (the ten and the head and to the end) is rendered hyper-literally; the precise sefirotic reference is left as written. In the passage on the Kaddish, 'הזכיר ה׳ עולמות כנגד ה׳ ספירות' — 'He mentioned HaShem, worlds, corresponding to five sefirot' — is rendered exactly; 'ה׳' before ספירות is written with the abbreviation mark and read as the number five."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:40.842132055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}